# L §5.18: "`for` binds keys: one line per binding tuple whose `where` holds. A tuple whose `where` is a hole prints its
# line as that hole, its value unevaluated, and so does a `diff` line in the world where its `where` is a hole."
# lim has no row at kb in the base, so lim[kb] > 0 is a hole there: q's line for kb is that hole (v[kb] = 20 is not
# printed). In sc lim[kb] = 1, so the where holds; d's line for kb holds 20 in sc and the hole in base (L §5.18's diff
# row: "changed with both values"); ka is 10 in both worlds, counted unchanged. (Writer 6.)
=== base.tally
model base
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2028-12-31

source s "s"
index ks = [ka, kb]
input lim[k in ks]: int
input v[k in ks]: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2025-12-01
  lim[ka] = 1
  v[ka] = 10
  v[kb] = 20

ask q: v[k] for k in ks where lim[k] > 0 law 2027-06-01
=== sc.tally
scenario sc over base
  tally 2
  from 2027-01-01

version w
  cite s
  governs 2027..
  known 2026-06-01
  lim[kb] = 1

ask d: diff(v[k] for k in ks where lim[k] > 0) in sc against base law 2027-06-01
=== expect
d: diff in sc against base: 1 changed, 0 added, 0 removed, 1 unchanged
  changed (k = kb): 20 against ?"lim at kb ...
...
q (k = ka): 10
...
q (k = kb): ?"lim at kb ...
